# java-script<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Home Automation</title>

  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial;
      text-align: center;
      background: #f2f2f2;
    }

    h1 {
      color: #333;
    }

    button {
      width: 150px;
      padding: 15px;
      margin: 10px;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 8px;
      font-size: 18px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    .on {
      background: green;
      color: white;
    }

    .off {
      background: red;
      color: white;
    }
  </style>
</head>

<body>

  <h1>🏠 Home Automation</h1>

  <h2>Light</h2>
  <button class="on" onclick="lightOn()">ON</button>
  <button class="off" onclick="lightOff()">OFF</button>

  <h2>Fan</h2>
  <button class="on" onclick="fanOn()">ON</button>
  <button class="off" onclick="fanOff()">OFF</button>

  <script>
    function lightOn() {
      alert("Light ON");
    }

    function lightOff() {
      alert("Light OFF");
    }

    function fanOn() {
      alert("Fan ON");
    }

    function fanOff() {
      alert("Fan OFF");
    }
  </script>

</body>
</html>
:::

This is HTML + CSS + JavaScript. The buttons currently show alerts; to actually control appliances, we need to connect the JavaScript to your Arduino/ESP32/ESP8266.


